{
  "id": "80559",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Solubility and Complex Ion Equilibria: Summary",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "7nasc7wikkjzi06",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "eolir584zj0xvb1",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The equilibrium constant for an equilibrium involving the precipitation or dissolution of a slightly soluble ionic solid is called the solubility product, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "sp"
              },
              {
                "text": ", of the solid. When we have a heterogeneous equilibrium involving the slightly soluble solid M"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "p"
              },
              {
                "text": "X"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "q"
              },
              {
                "text": " and its ions M"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "m+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and X"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "n−"
              },
              {
                "text": ": "
              }
            ],
            "id": "czbdkz5hgm5z2et",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "pg1tds5eu150mjq",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9lbr9joc0hjeyp9",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm Mp\\mathrm Xq(s)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;p\\mathrm M^{\\mathrm m+}(aq)\\;+\\;q\\mathrm X^{\\mathrm n-}(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We write the solubility product expression as:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "dgcgv1kr1by74gb",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "zzt76703fqo2qml",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9w1azxtyow3i4hi",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "K_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\;=\\;\\lbrack\\mathrm M^{\\mathrm m+}\\rbrack^p\\lbrack\\mathrm X^{\\mathrm n-}\\rbrack^q",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The solubility product of a slightly soluble electrolyte can be calculated from its solubility; conversely, its solubility can be calculated from its "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "sp"
              },
              {
                "text": " provided the only significant reaction that occurs when the solid dissolves is the formation of its ions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "b79p62ngn9cpfyh",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A slightly soluble electrolyte begins to precipitate when the magnitude of the reaction quotient for the dissolution reaction exceeds the magnitude of the solubility product. Precipitation continues until the reaction quotient equals the solubility product."
              }
            ],
            "id": "p78wzdj3sgf82aq",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A reagent can be added to a solution of ions to allow one ion to selectively precipitate out of solution. The common ion effect can also play a role in precipitation reactions. In the presence of an ion in common with one of the ions in the solution, Le Châtelier’s principle applies and more precipitate comes out of solution so that the molar solubility is reduced."
              }
            ],
            "id": "d4i82uirm9vbosr",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In a complex ion, we have a central atom, often consisting of a transition metal cation, which acts as a Lewis acid, and several neutral molecules or ions surrounding them, called ligands, that act as Lewis bases. Complex ions form by sharing electron pairs to form coordinate covalent bonds. The equilibrium reaction that occurs when forming a complex ion has an equilibrium constant associated with it called a formation constant, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": ". This is often referred to as a "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "stability constant"
              },
              {
                "text": ", as it represents the stability of the complex ion. Formation of complex ions in solution can have a profound effect on the solubility of a transition metal compound."
              }
            ],
            "id": "cnv0fn9998wqg49",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "myi83fruwcinpp9",
                "text": "End of Lesson Self-Assessment"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4fzp9db7ars6fcj",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
